1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,143
Coach: You do not need to repair a whole discussion in one turn.

2
00:00:05,143 --> 00:00:09,000
Coach: A useful next move can identify a missing link,

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,286
Coach: ask a manageable question, or give someone room to continue.

4
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:19,286
Coach: Choose what the moment needs instead of trying to demonstrate every technique at once.

5
00:00:19,636 --> 00:00:22,636
Coach: This rehearsal starts from a fresh excerpt.

6
00:00:22,636 --> 00:00:28,208
Coach: The group is considering the twenty-minute welcome activity in the main room,

7
00:00:28,208 --> 00:00:30,350
Coach: using existing paper and pens.

8
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:32,065
Coach: Both aims still matter:

9
00:00:32,065 --> 00:00:36,779
Coach: meeting one another and finding out about the weekly drawing group.

10
00:00:36,779 --> 00:00:38,922
Coach: No proposal has been adopted.

11
00:00:39,272 --> 00:00:43,558
Maya: We could start in pairs and exchange names and interests.

12
00:00:46,908 --> 00:00:48,622
Coach: A brief pause follows.

13
00:00:48,622 --> 00:00:50,765
Coach: We do not know why.

14
00:00:50,765 --> 00:00:57,194
Coach: In this excerpt, nobody has yet connected the paired introduction to the weekly drawing group.

15
00:00:57,194 --> 00:01:00,622
Coach: That gives you a particular gap to consider,

16
00:01:00,622 --> 00:01:07,051
Coach: without needing to diagnose how anyone feels or decide that silence itself is a problem.

17
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:12,544
Coach: You could build on Maya’s idea by asking about that second aim.

18
00:01:12,544 --> 00:01:18,115
Coach: You could also leave a little space if someone seems about to continue,

19
00:01:18,115 --> 00:01:20,687
Coach: without claiming to know their intention.

20
00:01:20,687 --> 00:01:27,544
Coach: There is no rule that a certain number of quiet seconds means you must take over.

21
00:01:27,894 --> 00:01:33,894
Coach: For this rehearsal, prepare one short line that would help examine the missing link.

22
00:01:33,894 --> 00:01:37,323
Coach: Keep it connected to the proposal already offered.

23
00:01:37,323 --> 00:01:42,894
Coach: You are not choosing a final activity, making up the drawing group’s programme,

24
00:01:42,894 --> 00:01:47,180
Coach: or promising that every new member will learn about it.

25
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:57,673
Coach: Try the line in English.

26
00:01:57,673 --> 00:02:02,816
Coach: You can say it aloud, write it privately, or think it through.

27
00:02:02,816 --> 00:02:06,673
Coach: This is private practice, with no recording or scoring.

28
00:02:06,673 --> 00:02:10,101
Coach: Aim for something the group could respond to,

29
00:02:10,101 --> 00:02:15,244
Coach: then leave a little space instead of immediately answering your own question.

30
00:02:15,594 --> 00:02:18,165
Coach: Watch gives you thirty-five seconds,

31
00:02:18,165 --> 00:02:21,166
Coach: then shows one example and continues automatically.

32
00:02:21,166 --> 00:02:25,880
Coach: Practice waits for you to see the model or skip it.

33
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:30,594
Coach: In Practice, after the model you can try again or continue.

34
00:02:30,594 --> 00:02:34,880
Coach: The example is one possibility, not the only useful response.

35
00:03:10,230 --> 00:03:13,230
You: The paired introductions could help people meet.

36
00:03:13,230 --> 00:03:18,801
You: How could they also help members find out about the weekly drawing group?

37
00:03:19,151 --> 00:03:22,579
Noah: Pairs could write one question about the group.

38
00:03:22,579 --> 00:03:27,722
Noah: We would still need to check where the answers would come from.

39
00:03:28,072 --> 00:03:34,072
Coach: The next move picks up Maya’s idea and draws attention to the missing aim.

40
00:03:34,072 --> 00:03:37,501
Coach: Noah’s suggestion makes the information question more specific.

41
00:03:37,501 --> 00:03:42,215
Coach: Writing a question does not yet tell visitors about the group;

42
00:03:42,215 --> 00:03:44,787
Coach: an answer source still needs checking.

43
00:03:44,787 --> 00:03:49,501
Coach: The model leaves that work open rather than finishing the plan.

44
00:03:49,851 --> 00:03:54,565
Coach: Check whether your next move responds to what was actually said

45
00:03:54,565 --> 00:03:57,565
Coach: and gives the group a manageable question.

46
00:03:57,565 --> 00:04:02,708
Coach: If you did not make an attempt, keep those checks for later.

47
00:04:02,708 --> 00:04:08,708
Coach: You do not have to supply the final activity before making a useful contribution.

48
00:04:09,058 --> 00:04:12,058
Coach: What comes next depends on the reply.

49
00:04:12,058 --> 00:04:17,201
Coach: Someone might suggest a connection, ask for clarification, or still need time.

50
00:04:17,201 --> 00:04:21,915
Coach: Attend to that response rather than forcing your prepared follow-up.

51
00:04:21,915 --> 00:04:25,773
Coach: A question that sounds polite does not guarantee agreement

52
00:04:25,773 --> 00:04:27,487
Coach: or a smooth continuation.

53
00:04:27,837 --> 00:04:30,837
Coach: For another attempt, choose one small change:

54
00:04:30,837 --> 00:04:35,980
Coach: name the missing aim more clearly, shorten a question with several demands,

55
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:38,980
Coach: or remove an assumption about the group.

56
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:43,694
Coach: Keep your line open enough for someone else to develop it.

57
00:04:43,694 --> 00:04:46,694
Coach: You can leave a useful problem unresolved.

58
00:04:59,044 --> 00:05:03,330
Coach: A small repair gives the group a place to continue.

59
00:05:03,330 --> 00:05:06,758
Coach: Offer it, leave space, and notice what follows.

60
00:05:06,758 --> 00:05:12,330
Coach: The eventual recommendation still needs to serve both aims within the activity’s conditions;

61
00:05:12,330 --> 00:05:16,187
Coach: this short contribution does not settle that wider decision.
